howard.disk
-----------

The system pack of the AGT-30 at North Carolina State University's
graphics lab, as dumped to tape in April 1976: the monitor AMRMX (the
AMOS/2 disk monitor) with its overlays and symbol table, and volume 10
with the games LIFE, QUBIC, Space War (SPCWR) and BROSE, among others.
The simulator boots AMRMX from it and starts the games
(research/amrmx.md, research/ncsu-tapes.md).

  Size     409344 bytes: 102,336 words of 30 bits, four bytes each,
           low byte first; the pack's first 984 sectors in order
  SHA-256  1b9a60b8efe47a29d772880e159d6c988d6e118070a397ea78fce19d8aa5c56c

Copied unchanged from M. Howard's repository, where it is made from the
tape image 556_howard_sys_pack by his tools:

  https://github.com/cramcram/AGT-30
  (commit 235e2b43f1f4c7b52c9a15b94aba107d89e8522b, magtapes/howard.disk)


games.tape
----------

A tape of the lab's games, written in January 1975: QUBIC, LIFE, BROSE
and Space War (SPCWR) as core images, with the display part of 3-D
tic-tac-toe, BCOR, CDAF and 9SE, the set written twice. The pack's
copy of BROSE is cut short where the dump ends; the simulator loads
BROSE from this tape (research/ncsu-tapes.md).

  Size     436264 bytes: AMOS tape records, each 30-bit word in four
           bytes, low byte first (js/sim/amostape.js)
  SHA-256  66d9493879a26a3df3c25a81edf81e09d4cffb69f1bbf49394285d8fbd196d88

Copied unchanged from M. Howard's repository, where it is made from the
tape image games_1975jan by his tools:

  https://github.com/cramcram/AGT-30
  (commit 235e2b43f1f4c7b52c9a15b94aba107d89e8522b, magtapes/games.tape)


Permission and status
---------------------

M. Howard, who recovered the tapes, agreed to their use in this
simulator (a call on 2026-11-19, and his message of 2026-11-28;
research/correspondence.md). Adage, Inc. ceased operations in 1994. No
license to redistribute this software has been granted by its
copyright holders, and none is claimed here. It is included as a
historical artifact, for study and for running on a simulator of the
machine it was written for. If you are a rights holder and would rather
it were not here, please open an issue on the repository and it will be
removed.

If you would rather not have these files in your copy of the
repository, delete them. The simulator's own programs run without them;
NCSU's monitor and games will not load.
